# sentinel — the operator's file. Copy to .env on Storage-NAS and edit.
#
# deploy.sh creates .env from this file ONCE, on first deploy, and
# never overwrites it afterwards. That is deliberate: several of these values are
# judgements made against the fleet as it is (the two media paths, the ssh key
# path, the bind address), and a deploy that reset them would silently revert a
# correction. `--status` reports which of these are still at their defaults.
#
# NOTHING IN HERE IS A SECRET BY ITSELF. The ssh key is a FILE mounted into the
# container, not a value in this file; there is no password to this stack. If TVH
# credentials are ever supplied below, they become the only secret here and this
# file's permissions are what protect them.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Paths on Storage-NAS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where this stack's own directory lives. The SQLite store goes in ./data under
# it. Must be on the NAS's own filesystem, never a network share.
MONITOR_DATA=/share/CACHEDEV1_DATA/Programs/sentinel/data

# The pvr stack's media directory. recordings and transcoded are the SAME
# filesystem -- a full volume stops recording and transcoding together, which is
# why the dashboard treats it as one critical metric rather than two.
PVR_DATA=/share/CACHEDEV1_DATA/Programs/pvr

# THE PATHS INSIDE THE CONTAINER ARE NOT THE PATHS ON THE HOST, so these two
# must be set explicitly. The compose file mounts the tree above at /mnt/, and
# config.py's defaults are the HOST paths, which do not exist in here. Leaving
# these at their defaults is the kind of mistake that produces a permanent "the
# media volume does not exist" RED on a fleet whose media is fine.
RECORDINGS_PATH=/mnt/recordings
TRANSCODED_PATH=/mnt/transcoded

TZ=Europe/Helsinki


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The dashboard
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The container's OWN LAN address on the `eth1` macvlan network, matching how
# every other service on Storage-NAS is addressed. The dashboard is reached at
# http://${LOCAL_IPV4}:8787 and the container publishes NO host port.
#
# .51 IS DELIBERATELY A NEW ALLOCATION, not a reuse. Checked free 2026-09-26:
# no ping reply and no ARP entry, and it appears in no reservation. The
# neighbours are .49 (the host's eth1 services address), .50 (host ssh/smb),
# .52/.53/.54/.56/.59/.61/.62/.63/.70 (live containers), .55 and .58 (reserved
# for hermes/nginx), .60 (Backup-NAS) and .64-.95 (Docker's DHCP pool, which
# must not be touched). .57 is labelled openclaw but openclaw runs on a bridge,
# so it was passed over: a name-reserved address can collide the day its owner
# actually claims it.
LOCAL_IPV4=198.51.100.11
MONITOR_PORT=8787

# How often the whole fleet is collected. 60 s is the design point: the fleet's
# fastest-changing signals (a pass starting, a tuner event) are minute-scale, and
# a shorter interval would mostly add load to Backup-NAS, which has 57 MB free
# and a load average of 2.2 on one core.
MONITOR_INTERVAL=60

# Client-side ssh bound, per call. This is a REAL bound, not a hint: the CuBoxes'
# state mount is mounted HARD, so a stat against a dead Backup-NAS blocks in
# D state, which no server-side timeout and no signal can kill. Raising this
# slows the fleet's discovery of a dead host; lowering it below ~10 s starts
# producing timeouts on a merely busy box, which read as UNKNOWN.
MONITOR_SSH_TIMEOUT=25


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the monitor may DO, as opposed to what it may see
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Remedies are TIER 2 ONLY: a remedy runs only when every precondition is
# POSITIVELY observed, and any `unknown` in that set means no remedy. There are
# deliberately very few, and the ones that exist are documented in
# docs/architecture.md along with the ones that were removed and why --
# a `rm -f *.part` sweep and a restart-on-NRestarts were both designed, then
# deleted, because each reproduced the silent failure it was written to cure.
#
# Leaving this off is a valid permanent choice: the dashboard still shows every
# incident, and every remedy that would have run is still described on the page.
MONITOR_REMEDIES=0

# Collect and evaluate but write NOTHING to the store -- not a single row, not
# even a check outcome. Used for the first run against a live fleet, and for
# testing a threshold change without leaving a false pass record behind (item 55:
# the mode whose contract is "claims nothing" has to actually claim nothing).
MONITOR_DRY_RUN=0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The fleet
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Both CuBoxes, and the two hosts they depend on. Addresses rather than ssh
# aliases: there is no ~/.ssh/config in the container, and an alias that resolves
# on the operator's laptop and not in here is a failure that only appears after
# deployment.
#MONITOR_HOST_CUBOX_1_ADDR=198.51.100.31
#MONITOR_HOST_CUBOX_1_USER=root
#MONITOR_HOST_CUBOX_2_ADDR=198.51.100.32
#MONITOR_HOST_CUBOX_2_USER=root
#MONITOR_HOST_BACKUP_ADDR=198.51.100.20
#MONITOR_HOST_BACKUP_USER=admin

# The TVH container, as `docker ps` reports it, and the DVB adapters the
# WinTV-dualHD exposes. NOTE the second tuner currently refuses recordings
# (operator-reported): that is modelled as a KNOWN-ACCEPTED condition with a
# dated acknowledgement, not as a fault, because a check that is RED from day one
# trains the operator to ignore red. Presence is checked; function is only
# asserted when a real recording has happened.
TVH_CONTAINER=tvheadend
DVB_ADAPTERS=adapter0,adapter1

# How much of the TVH container log one collection reads. This is a WINDOW SIZE,
# not a detail: the DVR-pairing check pairs a recording's subscribe against its
# unsubscribe, so a tail shorter than one recording cannot see both ends and must
# report UNKNOWN rather than green.
TVH_LOG_TAIL=20000


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TVH's own API -- OPTIONAL, and currently INERT. Read the whole note.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Not needed for the DVB checks: the container log carries adapter and DVR
# events, and that is where they are read from.
#
# NOTHING READS THESE YET, SO SETTING THEM CHANGES NO VERDICT. They are parsed
# into `cfg.tvh_user` / `cfg.tvh_pass`, and the ONLY consumer in the tree is
# `tvh_response_ms`, which records `auth_configured: bool(cfg.tvh_user)` in its
# EVIDENCE and never authenticates with them -- `probes.http_probe()` takes no
# credentials at all. Verified 2026-10-04 by grepping the whole monitor tree.
# This note previously promised "per-tuner signal quality", which was never
# implemented; an operator who set these expecting a change would find none and
# no explanation for it.
#
# What they CANNOT fix, and why the 401 is not a defect. TVH has auth on, so
# `tvh_response_ms` gets 401 and reports UNKNOWN -- deliberately, because `curl
# -f` would make that a PERMANENT RED while "any HTTP code came back" would make
# it a permanent GREEN on a service that answered nothing. Note also that a
# WRONG password returns the same 401 as NO password, so "the credentials work"
# needs its own validity probe and must never be inferred from an answer.
#
# A consequence worth knowing before you wait for it: UNKNOWN does not resolve an
# incident (store.py -- only a positive observation does), so a TVH incident
# opened while it was RED stays open for as long as this check stays grey, and
# no configuration closes it. That is deliberate.
#
# To use these, extend the probe to authenticate AND distinguish "authenticated,
# real status" from "401, wrong-or-absent password". Until then: leave them
# commented out.
#TVH_USER=
#TVH_PASS=
